  {
    "pkattachmentId": 38921,
    "bookid": 36,
    "chapterId": 275,
    "fileId": 20877,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Aspects related to the etiopathogenesis of MALT lymphoma of the genitourinary tract</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Kikkeri N. Naresh",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"border-collapse: collapse; border: none;\" border=\"1\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 163.05pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"217\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Agent/aspect</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 8.0cm; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"302\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Association</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 308.05pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"411\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Additional information</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 163.05pt; border: none; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"217\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Infective agents, e.g. <em>E.&nbsp;coli</em></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 8.0cm; border: none; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"302\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Cystitis or prostatitis</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 308.05pt; border: none; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"411\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Tumour regresses after treatment of infection with antibiotics</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 163.05pt; border: none; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"217\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Autoimmune</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 8.0cm; border: none; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"302\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Autoimmune/interstitial cystitis</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 308.05pt; border: none; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"411\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">&ndash;</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid; height: 53.2pt;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 163.05pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 53.2pt;\" width=\"217\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Structural and numeric chromosomal changes in common with other MALT lymphomas, investigated using FISH studies on paraffin sections</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 8.0cm; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 53.2pt;\" width=\"302\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">t(11;18)(q21;q21) (<em>BIRC3</em>&nbsp;[<em>API2</em>]::<em>MALT1</em>)&nbsp;&ndash; urinary bladder</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">t(14;18)(q32;q21) (IGH::<em>MALT1</em>)&nbsp;&ndash; kidney</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Trisomy&nbsp;3&nbsp;&ndash; urinary bladder</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 308.05pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 53.2pt;\" width=\"411\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">It may also be useful to investigate for t(3;14)(p14.1;q32) (<em>FOXP1</em>::IGH) and t(1;14)(p22;q32) (<em>BCL10</em>::IGH), as well as for trisomy&nbsp;18, because these abnormalities are seen in MALT lymphomas at other sites</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 38922,
    "bookid": 36,
    "chapterId": 275,
    "fileId": 20878,
    "type": "Table",
    "diagnosis": "",
    "title": "<p>Key differential diagnoses of MALT lymphoma in the genitourinary tract</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Kikkeri N. Naresh",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 697.95pt; border-collapse: collapse; border: none;\" border=\"1\" width=\"931\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 165.75pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"221\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Entity</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 259.5pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"346\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black;\">Morphology</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 272.7pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"364\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black;\">Key additional features</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 165.75pt; border: none; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"221\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Small lymphocyte&ndash;rich and plasma cell&ndash;rich reactive infiltrates</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 259.5pt; border: none; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"346\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Lymphoid follicles with germinal centres; mature plasma cells</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 272.7pt; border: none; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"364\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Polytypic light chain expression; polyclonal IG gene rearrangement; also investigate for excess IgG4+ plasma cells</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 165.75pt; border: none; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"221\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black;\">Other small B-cell lymphomas</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 259.5pt; border: none; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"346\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black;\">Some overlap with MALT lymphoma in morphological features</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 272.7pt; border: none; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"364\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black;\">MALT lymphomas typically lack expression of CD5, CD10, BCL6, cyclin&nbsp;D1, SOX11, and LEF1; lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma and Waldenstr&ouml;m macroglobulinaemia are positive for <em>MYD88</em> mutation</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 165.75pt; border: none; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"221\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Extramedullary plasmacytoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 259.5pt; border: none; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"346\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Plasma cell infiltrate that is not accompanied by small B&nbsp;cells; plasma cells may show variable atypia</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 272.7pt; border: none; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"364\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Cells showing plasmacytoid differentiation in MALT lymphomas do not show expression of CD56, KIT (CD117), or cyclin&nbsp;D1, and these may be expressed by plasma cells of extramedullary plasmacytoma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 165.75pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"221\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong><span style=\"color: black;\">Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</span></strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 259.5pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"346\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black;\">Sheets of large nucleolated cells variably admixed with other inflammatory cells including reactive small lymphocytes; cases with variability in the size of the neoplastic cells have &gt;&nbsp;20% large nucleolated cells</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 272.7pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"364\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><span style=\"color: black;\">Ki-67 expression is seen in &gt;&nbsp;50% (often &gt;&nbsp;80%) of cells</span></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
  {
    "pkattachmentId": 38923,
